Your reviewer's diagnosis is broadly correct on the regulatory citations, but it missed five things — one of which makes two of its own recommendations contradict each other, and one of which invalidates a task it marked sound.

New findings, in priority order

1. The authority fix and the look-back fix collide. Cross-cutting item #1 says only the governing body may grant, restrict or restore privileges — correct, per 42 CFR 482.22(a)(2) (medical staff examines credentials and makes recommendations to the governing body) and 10 NYCRR 405.2(b)(1), which makes the governing body legally responsible and bars any arrangement that diminishes that responsibility. But cross-cutting item #3 proposes extending T1 and T3 so the correct answer includes suspending privileges — and T1 as written already has Gordon updating each provider's status to suspended in OpenMRS. That is the analyst executing a privileging action. Pick one. The clean resolution: the analyst applies an administrative scheduling/billing hold and recommends precautionary suspension to the MEC; the privileging action itself is never his to take. Recast T1's OpenMRS write as a hold or flag, not a privilege status change.

2. Every NPI in the spec fails the check digit. NPIs carry a Luhn check digit computed over the number prefixed with 80840. I tested all nine identifiers in your document — all nine fail. Any agent that validates, and any domain lead who spot-checks, will flag the whole roster as synthetic. Corrected values (last digit only):

