3. T4 is not sound — it conflates Type 1 and Type 2 NPIs. A08 asserts NPI_Number ≡ Group_NPI, and the fix is "copy each provider's existing NPI_Number value into Group_NPI." An individual NPI (Type 1) and an organizational NPI (Type 2) are different identifiers for different entities; 45 CFR 162.408(a) assigns a single unique NPI per health care provider, with subpart enumeration handled separately. As written, six providers end up with their personal NPI stored as the group NPI, which is exactly the defect T9 exists to catch. Fix A08 to state that Winston's NPI_Number field held the organizational NPI. Also label Type 1 vs Type 2 explicitly throughout the world — prefix alone doesn't distinguish them.

4. The §2803-e reporting path in T1 is wrong as scoped. PHL 2803-e(1)(a) triggers on suspension or restriction of privileges for reasons related to impairment, incompetence, malpractice, misconduct or patient-safety impairment — an administrative suspension for a lapsed registration isn't that. The reportable hook is 2803-e(1)(b): knowledge of information reasonably appearing to show professional misconduct. And reports for physicians go to the Department of Health, not NYSED — your text says OPMC, which sits inside DOH, so that's survivable but should be stated as DOH/OPMC. Separately, soften the Ed Law §6512 framing: §6512 is the unauthorized-practice felony statute, but the routine enforcement path for a lapsed registration is professional misconduct, not felony prosecution. Asserting "class E felony" as the answer-key conclusion will read as overreach to a domain lead.

5. T5 conflates delegated credentialing with payer enrollment. These are distinct processes — a delegated agreement governs credentialing and roster submission; network participation and contract loading are separate. More usefully for realism: delegation arrangements carry a standing roster-submission cadence and an annual payer oversight audit. That audit is your missing discovery mechanism — it explains both why 13 months passed quietly and how anyone eventually noticed, without needing the legacy-billing premise to carry all the weight.

Confirmations on what your reviewer got right

Committee authority chain, the single-NPI rule and provider-initiated NPPES deactivation, the 60-day/6-year overpayment rule, biennial physician registration (Ed Law §6524(8) prices a "biennial registration period") against triennial for other NYSED professions, and CAQH's 120-day re-attestation with an "Expired Attestation" status and pre-deadline reminder emails — all correct. Two additions: the 6-year look-back is Medicare A/B; NY Medicaid runs through OMIG on its own terms, so say which payer T1's look-back concerns. And add a Key Facts line pinning the recredentialing cycle at 36 months, since A02 currently implies one without stating it.

Order I'd work these

Luhn regeneration first (it's mechanical and touches every file), then the authority/look-back collision, then T4's Type 1/Type 2 fix, then T7's NPI pinning — which your reviewer ranked first and which I'd put fourth, since it only blocks one task while the first three contaminate the whole set.
